One change to the recommendation. RIDER_OPTIONS has no list for 'frame' (RIDER_DEFAULT.frame is '#fff'), so a check of "each key must be in RIDER_OPTIONS" would always reset frame to its default. riderNorm should do three things:
- check the enum maps (skin, hairStyle, pattern, face, glasses) by key;
- check the colour lists by value;
- check frame, and any colour outside the lists, against /^#[0-9a-f]{3,6}$/i.

Do this in restoreAll next to :14581, in syncSetSettings (:17631) and on load (:8555). The main threat is a crafted backup; a synced-doc attack needs write access to the user's own document.

notes:
REAL: The first half is right. In origin/main, JHP_STYLES at :14072 is ['stripes','photo','photo-stripes']. jhpNorm (:14075-14077) turns any other style into 'stripes', and it also resets style/x/y/zoom to stripes whenever photoAt is 0. A World header needs no photo, so storing {style:'world'} in settings.journalHeader would read back as stripes through jhpGet, jhpSet, jhpMerge and jhpShown in the 101 build itself, unless jhpNorm is changed. HANDOFF.md item 101 (line 113-114) only says "A World option for the Journal header", and section E adds no key for it, so the plan really doesn't say where the choice lives. This is a real build note.

The second half, "older builds push stripes back", mostly does not hold. jhpDocMerge (:14084) compares the merged result with jhpNorm(d.journalHeader), and both sides are normalised. When the cloud's World copy wins (newer updatedAt, equal photoAt/goneAt), m equals the normalised d. So d comes back unchanged, keeps the raw 'world' value, and nothing goes up at :17578. At :17581 the old build uploads its raw settings.journalHeader, which is still 'world'. q96's sfDocMerge (wt-q96 index.html:17546) calls the same jhpDocMerge, so it behaves the same way. An old build only shows stripes. It writes stripes back only in edge cases:
- the user edits the header on that device (jhpSet normalises);
- the two copies have different goneAt or photoAt (for example a device with an old photo);
- the old device's header copy has the newer updatedAt.
I checked this by tracing the code, not by running a sync. Severity is low to medium (a build pitfall), not a sync-corruption problem. | correction: Keep: if World is stored as journalHeader.style it gets wiped, because 'world' is not in JHP_STYLES (main :14072) and jhpNorm turns a header with no photo into stripes (main :14077). So item 101 must either store the choice in its own key (for example settings.journalWorld, read with a fallback) or add 'world' to JHP_STYLES and exempt it from the no-photo reset. Drop or soften "older builds push stripes back". jhpDocMerge returns the cloud copy unchanged when the merged value equals its own normalised form (main :14084, :17578; q96 sfDocMerge uses the same call). So a pre-101 build keeps the raw 'world' value and only shows stripes. It writes stripes back only when someone edits the header on that build, or when the copies' goneAt/photoAt or updatedAt differ in its favour. A separate key avoids even those cases.
